How the MVP flow works
1. Customer defines an agent

They write something like:

import { defineAgent, tool } from "@yourco/agent-sdk";

export const cpaAssistant = defineAgent({
  name: "cpa-assistant",
  model: "anthropic/claude-sonnet",
  system: `
    You are a CPA workflow assistant.
    Help prepare and review filings.
    Use tools when needed.
  `,
  tools: [
    tool({
      name: "lookupClient",
      description: "Get client profile by ID",
      inputSchema: {
        type: "object",
        properties: {
          clientId: { type: "string" }
        },
        required: ["clientId"]
      },
      execute: async ({ clientId }, ctx) => {
        return await ctx.http.post("/internal/lookup-client", { clientId });
      }
    })
  ]
});
2. Customer backend creates a session
const session = await agentClient.sessions.create({
  agent: "cpa-assistant",
  userId: "user_123",
  metadata: {
    firmId: "firm_456"
  }
});

This returns:

sessionId
short-lived clientToken
websocket URL or stream endpoint
3. Frontend connects and sends messages
const chat = createChatSession({
  sessionId,
  token: clientToken
});

chat.sendMessage({
  role: "user",
  content: "Show me open tasks for client 1042."
});
4. Your runtime processes the turn

Internally:

store user message
choose provider/model
stream assistant tokens
if tool call appears:
emit tool_call_started
invoke tool
emit tool_call_result
resume model
finalize assistant message
persist all events
5. Frontend receives structured stream events

Not just raw text.

Something like:

type ChatEvent =
  | { type: "message_started"; messageId: string }
  | { type: "token"; messageId: string; text: string }
  | { type: "tool_call_started"; toolName: string; callId: string }
  | { type: "tool_call_completed"; toolName: string; callId: string; resultPreview?: string }
  | { type: "message_completed"; messageId: string }
  | { type: "run_failed"; error: string };

This is important because it gives the frontend a rich UX.

