What transport should MVP use?

For MVP, I would strongly consider:

Start with SSE, not WebSockets

Why:

easier infra
simpler auth story
simpler browser support
simpler horizontal scaling
enough for most chat apps

Pattern:

POST user message
open SSE stream for response events

If you truly need bidirectional realtime presence later, add WebSockets.

For many chat apps, SSE gets you 80% of the value with much less complexity.

So the client flow becomes:

POST /sessions/:id/messages
GET /sessions/:id/stream

That may be the right MVP.

What the hosted runtime actually does

The core loop inside your service is:

while (true) {
  const response = await provider.generate({
    model,
    messages,
    tools
  });

  streamPartialOutput(response);

  if (!response.toolCall) {
    saveFinalAssistantMessage(response);
    break;
  }

  const toolResult = await invokeTool(response.toolCall);

  saveToolCallAndResult(toolResult);

  messages.push(toolResultAsConversationTurn);
}

This is the real “agent orchestration” for MVP.

Simple, but useful.

Tool execution modes for MVP

You need one clean starting mode.

Best MVP: customer backend tool handlers

The customer tool executes in their backend process using your SDK.

That means:

secrets stay with them
internal APIs are easy
no public tool callback mess at first
very low trust barrier

So the runtime can call into their registered tool endpoint or lightweight runner.

Two viable MVP options
Option A: tool webhook

Customer hosts tool endpoint(s).

You call:

POST /tools/lookupClient
Option B: lightweight agent runner

Customer runs a tiny process that registers tools and polls for tool calls.

Honestly, for this product, tool webhook first may be simplest.

